WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:06.700
Disney movies have more than their fair share
of cool hidden Easter eggs.

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:11.660
They also happen to have lots of weird, controversial,
and just plain creepy secrets and moments

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:15.969
that only someone who's truly obsessed would
bother compiling into a single list.

00:00:15.969 --> 00:00:17.560
So here we go.

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:21.020
The Lion King's sex dust

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:25.390
Remember watching 1994's The Lion King as
a kid, being enthralled by Simba's struggle

00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:29.470
to face his fears and confront his villainous
uncle, and being totally distracted by the

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:31.670
sudden and unmistakable appearance of SEX?

00:00:31.670 --> 00:00:32.670
No?

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:36.900
Well, tell that to the American Life League,
the conservative group that in 1995 claimed

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:42.089
the word "SEX" is spelled out in a dust cloud
in the sky after Simba flops down onto a ledge.

00:00:42.089 --> 00:00:46.080
But Tom Sito, one of the movie's animators,
has since explained that the dust cloud actually

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:51.040
spells out "SFX," or "special effects," a
kind of a signature inserted into one frame

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:52.930
of animation by Disney's effects team.

00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:54.070
And you know?

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:55.250
That's probably true.

00:00:55.250 --> 00:01:00.670
Because slipping a word as tame as "sex" into
a single frame of an animated movie is unbelievably

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:01.800
lame.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:04.750
If an animator really wanted to mess with
people's heads, he'd put in something way

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:05.750
more risque.

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:08.640
Like "butt," or "winkle," or something.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:12.220
Or maybe he'd just throw in a picture of a
topless woman.

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:15.960
The Rescuers' topless woman picture

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:20.500
In 1999, Disney made a surprising announcement,
saying it was going to recall 3.4 million

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:25.930
copies of its 1977 animated film, The Rescuers,
just three days after the movie's second release

00:01:25.930 --> 00:01:26.930
on home video.

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:28.240
The reason?

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:32.180
That was even more shocking: hidden within
two frames of the movie was an inexplicable

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:34.230
photo of a naked lady.

00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:38.550
Apparently, sometime between the film's completion
and its debut in theaters, someone in the

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:42.840
distribution chain slipped the image in, unbeknownst
to Disney, or really anyone.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:46.390
The image probably would never have been discovered
at all if it weren't for improvements in video

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:50.030
technology that allowed people to freeze the
movie and find its secret shame.

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:53.960
Nearly three months later, The Rescuers was
released once again, this time without the

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:54.960
nudie pics.

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:59.060
Meanwhile, copies of the movie that escaped
the recall are fetching big bucks on ebay,

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:02.820
commanding whopping prices between, uh, four
and eleven dollars.

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:04.130
Never mind.

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:05.909
Anyway, this isn't really that bad.

00:02:05.909 --> 00:02:12.560
It's not like one of the animators drew a
woman not wearing any underpants.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:15.640
Jessica Rabbit isn't wearing underpants

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:20.560
In 1988's Who Framed Roger Rabbit?, femme
fatale Jessica Rabbit has an infamous line:

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:25.020
"I'm not bad, I'm just drawn that way."

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:28.270
As it turns out, that was a little truer than
anyone could've guessed.

00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:32.010
Just as the video release of The Rescuers
revealed a hidden controversy, so too did

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:34.530
the Laserdisc release of Roger Rabbit.

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:38.080
When Jessica and Eddie Valiant get into a
car crash, they're both thrown from their

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:39.080
cab.

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:42.250
For the briefest of moments, only about a
frame or two, viewers can see that the animators

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:46.210
declined to draw any definitive underwear
between Jessica's legs.

00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:48.490
So is a missing line of paint enough to cause
a panic?

00:02:48.490 --> 00:02:52.590
Is it creepy that Mickey owns a dog and is
friends with Goofy, who is also a dog?

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:54.819
The answer to both questions is "yes."

00:02:54.819 --> 00:02:57.680
Reports from the time said that retailers
sold out of the laserdisc for the movie one

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:01.420
day after initial news of the missing undies
hit the media back in 1994.

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:05.030
Fortunately for fans of decency everywhere,
the missing underthings were inserted for

00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:06.630
the movie's DVD release.

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:09.030
But come on, this was clearly much ado about
nothing.

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:15.770
Nobody could actually see any genitals or
anything.

00:03:15.770 --> 00:03:17.200
All the genitals in The Little Mermaid

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:21.540
The Little Mermaid from 1989 doesn't seem
like it'd have much room for any naughty bits,

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:24.980
especially since all of the movie's undersea
denizens have, well, people parts on the top

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:26.640
and fish parts on the bottom.

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:30.440
But when the evil Ursula tries to trick Eric
into marrying her, the minister officiating

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:33.950
the wedding seems to be...excited by the impending
nuptials.

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:37.640
Disney's Tom Sito said that what looks like
the guy's little minister is actually his

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:38.640
knee.

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:41.830
Then, on the cover art of the original home
video release for The Little Mermaid, many

00:03:41.830 --> 00:03:45.590
people have found what sure does look like
an important part of the male anatomy hidden

00:03:45.590 --> 00:03:47.970
in a spire on an underwater castle.

00:03:47.970 --> 00:03:50.940
One report claims that the artist, who was
a freelance illustrator working on the cover

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:54.530
and not a Disney employee, was simply in a
rush to finish the cover art and stayed up

00:03:54.530 --> 00:03:56.569
late the night before the deadline to finish
the job.

00:03:56.569 --> 00:04:00.769
In the end, the minister seems to have survived
various video releases untouched, while the

00:04:00.769 --> 00:04:02.900
cover image had its conspicuous spire cut.

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:06.099
Seriously, though, people are making mountains
out of molehills here.

00:04:06.099 --> 00:04:09.319
It'd make sense to get upset only if there
were something truly objectionable in these

00:04:09.319 --> 00:04:16.539
Disney movies...something like a never-ending
parade of stereotypes and blatant racism.

00:04:16.539 --> 00:04:19.890
The parade of stereotypes and blatant racism
in Disney movies

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:20.890
Okay!

00:04:20.890 --> 00:04:25.509
So, a whole lot of Disney movies were made
and released during a time in America where

00:04:25.509 --> 00:04:33.610
concepts like "ethnic and racial sensitivity"
or "political correctness" weren't really,

00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:35.279
like, things.

00:04:35.279 --> 00:04:39.840
As a result, some of these flicks have aged
pretty horribly due to the inclusion of characters

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:42.610
that are unavoidably offensive by today's
standards.

00:04:42.610 --> 00:04:48.560
In fact, one Disney movie is so entirely racist
that it will simply never be released on video.

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:49.689
Ever.

00:04:49.689 --> 00:04:50.740
Where to start?

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:55.639
In Dumbo from 1941, the baby elephant encounters
a group of crows, who've since been identified

00:04:55.639 --> 00:04:58.639
as racist caricatures of African-Americans.

00:04:58.639 --> 00:05:03.330
Case in point: the leader of that group was
originally called "Jim Crow" in the script.

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:06.580
However, some have pointed out that the crows
are actually positive, since they're some

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:09.749
of the only characters who help Dumbo and
identify with his outsider status.

00:05:09.749 --> 00:05:14.369
Too bad the rest of these examples aren't
quite so easy to justify.

00:05:14.369 --> 00:05:19.369
The original cut of Fantasia from 1940 features
a sequence based on Beethoven's Pastoral Symphony,

00:05:19.369 --> 00:05:23.349
which features centaurs and other Greek mythical
creatures getting ready for a feast.

00:05:23.349 --> 00:05:27.789
Included in that sequence are two servant
centaurs named Sunflower and Otika who are

00:05:27.789 --> 00:05:30.650
clearly modeled after African-American stereotypes.

00:05:30.650 --> 00:05:38.479
They've been cut from subsequent releases
of 

00:05:38.479 --> 00:05:40.759
the movie.

00:05:40.759 --> 00:05:45.999
Then there's the highly inappropriate depiction
of Native Americans in 1953's Peter Pan, in

00:05:45.999 --> 00:05:50.740
which Tiger Lily and her people act in ridiculously
wild ways and speak in broken English, and

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:53.280
are repeatedly referred to as "savages."

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:57.629
And Lady and the Tramp from 1955 features
an extended sequence depicting evil Siamese

00:05:57.629 --> 00:06:01.800
cats with thick accents and plenty of other
offensive Asian stereotypes.

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:06.599
The worst offender, though, is 1946's Song
of the South, a movie you may not even know

00:06:06.599 --> 00:06:09.569
about, since it's never been released on video
in the United States.

00:06:09.569 --> 00:06:12.879
You've definitely heard one of its tunes,
"Zip-a-Dee-Doo-Dah," which won the Academy

00:06:12.879 --> 00:06:20.210
Award for Best Song in 1947.

00:06:20.210 --> 00:06:24.509
But the movie's general reliance on black
stereotypes and racial caricatures have made

00:06:24.509 --> 00:06:29.399
it what one critic calls "one of Hollywood's
most resiliently offensive racist texts."

00:06:29.399 --> 00:06:30.399
Yikes.

00:06:30.399 --> 00:06:34.860
Kind of makes all those wieners and ding-dongs
all over The Little Mermaid sound almost cute

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:35.860
now, huh?

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:36.860
Thanks for watching!

00:06:36.860 --> 00:06:38.669
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to see more
videos like the one you just saw.

